//
// `reduce` folds into a TEXT accumulator (loft#951).
//
// `reduce` lowers to `acc = f(acc, x)` in a loop. A callee answering text is handed ONE
// caller-allocated buffer and CLEARS it on entry, so binding `acc` straight to that
// buffer means the next turn erases the fold so far. Measured before the fix:
// `--interpret` answered the LAST element ("c" for "abc"), and `--native` did not compile
// at all (`error[E0597]: var__reduce_acc_1 does not live long enough`). On released
// 2026.8.0 it was an internal compiler error earlier still.
//
// The cure is not new machinery: it is what the hand-written loop has always compiled to.
// An ordinary assignment whose right-hand side READS the variable being assigned routes
// through a SECOND, caller-owned work buffer — `OpClearText(w); OpAppendText(w, f(acc,
// x)); acc = w` — so the buffer the callee clears is never the one the live accumulator
// holds. Cell c9 is that loop, kept here as the oracle the fold is measured against.
//
// The accumulator also moved to FUNCTION scope. A text-valued block ends in a BORROW of
// its tail in generated Rust, so a block-scoped accumulator is dropped while still
// borrowed — which is what E0597 was saying. Every other text-valued block loft emits
// (a formatted string, say) already builds into a function-scope `__work_N`.
//
// Each cell answers a DISTINCT string, so a fold that collapsed to one implementation —
// or kept only its last step — is visible rather than merely compiling. The seed, the
// empty input and the single element are separate cells because they are the boundaries
// where "the seed" and "the fold" are told apart.

fn cat951(a: text, x: text) -> text { "{a}{x}" }

fn main() {
  xs = ["a", "b", "c", "d"];
  o = ["q"];

  // c1 — the filed shape: a text accumulator with an inline lambda. Four elements, not
  //      two: with the old lowering this answered "d", and a two-element input cannot
  //      tell "the last step" from "the last two".
  c1 = xs.reduce("", |a, x| { "{a}{x}" });
  assert(c1 == "abcd", "c1 = {c1}, want abcd");

  // c2 — a NAMED callback. Same fold, different callback kind: the buffer belongs to the
  //      callee's signature, not to how the callback was written.
  c2 = xs.reduce("", cat951);
  assert(c2 == "abcd", "c2 = {c2}, want abcd");

  // c3 — the free-function spelling must answer exactly what the method one does.
  c3 = reduce(xs, "", cat951);
  assert(c3 == "abcd", "c3 = {c3}, want abcd");

  // c4 — a NON-EMPTY seed. The seed is the accumulator's first value, so a fold that
  //      cleared the wrong buffer would lose it on the first turn rather than the last.
  c4 = xs.reduce("Z", cat951);
  assert(c4 == "Zabcd", "c4 = {c4}, want Zabcd");

  // c5 — EMPTY input: the answer is the seed, untouched, and the loop body never runs.
  e: vector<text> = [];
  c5 = e.reduce("seed", cat951);
  assert(c5 == "seed", "c5 = {c5}, want seed");

  // c6 — ONE element: the boundary where "kept only the last step" and "correct" agree,
  //      which is why it cannot be the only cell — but a fix that broke it would be
  //      invisible without it.
  c6 = o.reduce("", cat951);
  assert(c6 == "q", "c6 = {c6}, want q");

  // c7 (control) — a SCALAR accumulator, the shape that always worked. It takes no
  //      buffer at all, so it must be untouched by any of this.
  n = [1, 2, 3, 4];
  c7 = n.reduce(0, |a, x| { a + x });
  assert(c7 == 10, "c7 = {c7}, want 10");

  // c8 — TWO folds in one function. The second must not reuse the first's work buffer:
  //      they are separate sites and a shared buffer would leave the second holding the
  //      first's text.
  d1 = xs.reduce("", cat951);
  d2 = o.reduce("", cat951);
  assert(d1 == "abcd" && d2 == "q", "c8 = {d1}/{d2}, want abcd/q");

  // c9 (oracle) — the hand-written loop the fold must agree with. It went through the
  //      ordinary assignment machinery all along, which is why it was already right.
  acc = "";
  for x in xs { acc = cat951(acc, x); }
  assert(acc == "abcd", "c9 = {acc}, want abcd");

  // c10 — a fold whose callback IGNORES the accumulator. The result is the last element,
  //      which is also what the BUG produced — so this cell is only meaningful beside c1,
  //      and it pins that the fix did not simply hard-code "keep everything".
  c10 = xs.reduce("", |a, x| { "{x}" });
  assert(c10 == "d", "c10 = {c10}, want d");

  println("951 ok");
}
